{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_ecjbg6i7fayt4usc64x252qenqdnwjct3soyry4ampcluvvmoikq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01MGcTYbX3VZ7APCnvaMndA5","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-translate-BPbkRm/translated_rust/src/lib.rs","content":"//! Rust translation of the C library in `c_src/`.\n//!\n//! Public ABI (exactly matches `nm -D` on the C shared object):\n//!   * `tfm`\n//!\n//! # Bit-exactness\n//!\n//! `tfm` is pure `float` arithmetic, so reproducing the C library's output\n//! *byte for byte* requires reproducing not just the IEEE-754 values but also\n//! the **NaN payloads** the C build produces. Two things make that\n//! source-order-sensitive:\n//!\n//! 1. On x86 SSE, a binary op such as `addss %src, %dst` whose *both* operands\n//!    are NaN returns the (quieted) **destination** operand. Which C operand\n//!    lands in the destination register is a codegen choice: `fadd`/`fmul` are\n//!    commutative for values, so the compiler is free to swap them, and GCC\n//!    and LLVM make *different* choices here.\n//! 2. When an invalid operation creates a NaN from non-NaN operands\n//!    (`inf - inf`, `0 * inf`, `sqrt(-x)`), x86 returns the \"indefinite\" QNaN\n//!    `0xffc00000` — note the **set sign bit** — rather than `0x7fc00000`.\n//!\n//! Rather than hope the optimizer picks the same operand order, the arithmetic\n//! below routes every NaN-payload-sensitive operation through the `f*` helpers,\n//! which model x86 SSE propagation explicitly on raw bit patterns. The operand\n//! order handed to those helpers mirrors the reference C build's instruction\n//! selection, which differs from naive source order in three places:\n//!\n//! * `2.0f * dx2` is emitted as `dx2 + dx2` (`addss`, dst = `dx2`);\n//! * `dy2 + dx2` is hoisted out of both `if`/`else` arms and computed once as\n//!   `src[0] + src[1]` (dst = `src[0]`) — in the `if` arm that is a swap,\n//!   since there `dy2` is `src[1]`;\n//! * `(dy2 + dx2) + sqrtf(..)` is emitted with the **sqrt result** as the\n//!   destination, i.e. the sqrt's payload wins over the sum's.\n//!\n//! Because the helpers work on bits and never let a NaN reach a hardware FP\n//! instruction, the results are identical on any host, not just x86.\n//!\n//! No bugs are fixed: the inlined `max(0, sqd)` keeps its C semantics (it\n//! propagates NaN and does not normalize `-0.0`, unlike `f32::max`), the\n//! per-iteration expressions keep their C evaluation order, and a\n//! non-positive `count` still writes nothing.\n\n#![allow(clippy::missing_safety_doc)]\n\nuse core::ffi::c_int;\n\n/// The x86 \"real indefinite\" QNaN for `f32`, produced when an invalid\n/// operation manufactures a NaN out of non-NaN operands.\nconst INDEFINITE: u32 = 0xffc0_0000;\n\n/// Quiet-bit for an `f32` significand.\nconst QUIET_BIT: u32 = 0x0040_0000;\n\n#[inline(always)]\nfn is_nan_bits(x: u32) -> bool {\n    (x & 0x7f80_0000) == 0x7f80_0000 && (x & 0x007f_ffff) != 0\n}\n\n/// x86 quieting of a NaN operand: set the quiet bit, preserve sign and the\n/// rest of the payload. A QNaN passes through unchanged.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn quiet(x: u32) -> u32 {\n    x | QUIET_BIT\n}\n\n/// Applies x86 SSE NaN propagation to a two-operand arithmetic op.\n///\n/// `a` is the *destination* operand, so its payload takes precedence. Returns\n/// `Some(bits)` when the result is decided purely by NaN propagation.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn nan_result(a: u32, b: u32) -> Option<u32> {\n    if is_nan_bits(a) {\n        Some(quiet(a))\n    } else if is_nan_bits(b) {\n        Some(quiet(b))\n    } else {\n        None\n    }\n}\n\n/// Normalizes a NaN freshly manufactured from non-NaN operands to the x86\n/// indefinite QNaN.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn fresh(v: f32) -> u32 {\n    let bits = v.to_bits();\n    if is_nan_bits(bits) {\n        INDEFINITE\n    } else {\n        bits\n    }\n}\n\n/// `addss %b, %a` — computes `a + b` with `a` as the destination operand.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn fadd(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32 {\n    match nan_result(a, b) {\n        Some(r) => r,\n        None => fresh(f32::from_bits(a) + f32::from_bits(b)),\n    }\n}\n\n/// `subss %b, %a` — computes `a - b` with `a` as the destination operand.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn fsub(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32 {\n    match nan_result(a, b) {\n        Some(r) => r,\n        None => fresh(f32::from_bits(a) - f32::from_bits(b)),\n    }\n}\n\n/// `mulss %b, %a` — computes `a * b` with `a` as the destination operand.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn fmul(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32 {\n    match nan_result(a, b) {\n        Some(r) => r,\n        None => fresh(f32::from_bits(a) * f32::from_bits(b)),\n    }\n}\n\n/// `sqrtss` — quiets a NaN operand, yields the indefinite QNaN for a negative\n/// operand, and leaves `-0.0` as `-0.0`.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn fsqrt(a: u32) -> u32 {\n    if is_nan_bits(a) {\n        return quiet(a);\n    }\n    let v = f32::from_bits(a);\n    if v < 0.0 {\n        return INDEFINITE;\n    }\n    v.sqrt().to_bits()\n}\n\n/// `comiss`-style ordered `<`: false whenever either operand is NaN.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn flt(a: u32, b: u32) -> bool {\n    !is_nan_bits(a) && !is_nan_bits(b) && f32::from_bits(a) < f32::from_bits(b)\n}\n\nconst TWO: u32 = 0x4000_0000; // 2.0f  (used as dx2 + dx2)\nconst FOUR: u32 = 0x4080_0000; // 4.0f\nconst HALF: u32 = 0x3f00_0000; // 0.5f\n\n/// One loop iteration: reads three `float`s of input, writes two of output.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn step(s0: u32, s1: u32, s2: u32) -> (u32, u32) {\n    // Sub-expressions the reference build hoists out of the two arms.\n    let sq0 = fmul(s0, s0); // src[0] * src[0]\n    let sq1 = fmul(s1, s1); // src[1] * src[1]\n    let sum01 = fadd(s0, s1); // dy2 + dx2, with src[0] as destination\n    let dxy4 = fmul(fmul(s2, FOUR), s2); // (4.0f * dxy) * dxy\n\n    if flt(s0, s1) {\n        // src[0] < src[1]:  dx2 = src[0], dy2 = src[1], dxy = src[2]\n        let